From: usb/afc + IPA/sideload lane (2026-11-21)
Subject: 0505 sweet_hfs: do not clamp a fork whose tail lives in the extents overflow tree

The catalog reader validates a file fork by summing only the eight resident
extents, then, if that sum is shorter than the record's totalBlocks and the
eighth slot is empty, it marks the volume inconsistent and clamps totalBlocks
in memory (hfs_catalog.c, "Adjust for any missing blocks").  A fork whose tail
is in the extents overflow tree is perfectly consistent but matches that
condition, so every append-heavy log or trace file gets reported as runtime
corruption on every read and silently shortened.

Evidence on the repaired 128-GiB volume (2026-11-21): one 180-second boot
produced 13,405 "hfs_mark_inconsistent: Runtime corruption detected on iOSRoot"
messages while a post-boot allocation audit found zero overlap and zero
referenced-but-free blocks.  Two files (cnid 321405 logdata.statistics.0.txt,
cnid 1242857 ...tracev3) also had totalBlocks that did not match their extent
tree until salvaged by hand.

Fix: ask the extents overflow tree whether the continuation exists before
declaring corruption.  The predicate returns the conservative 0 (keep the old
clamp) when the extents B-tree is not loaded yet, so the early-mount path is
unchanged.  FindExtentRecord now also fails instead of dereferencing a NULL
iterator, because this read path can run under memory pressure.

Regression (next CI kernel + one bounded 180 s boot):
  * block_count_anomalies must stay 0 for files that grow during the boot;
  * hfs_mark_inconsistent must stay 0;
  * the 180 s timer must still return the phone to Android.

Signed-off-by: usb/afc + sideload lane
